<article class="env-remark" id="0722">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0722">Remark <span data-tag="0722">9.7.7.8</span>.</a> It follows immediately from the definitions that if a finite collection of $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete $\infty $-categories $\{  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_ i \} _{i \in I}$ admits a $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete tensor product, then that tensor product is uniquely determined up to equivalence. More precisely, suppose we are given $\mathbb {K}$-multilinear functors </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\xleftarrow {T} \prod _{i \in I} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_ i \xrightarrow {T'} \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}', \]
</div>
<p> where $T$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ as a $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete tensor product of $\{  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_ i \} _{i \in I }$. It follows from the universal property of Definition <a data-tag="0720" href="/tag/0720">9.7.7.6</a> that there exists a $\mathbb {K}$-cocontinuous functor $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}'$ such that $U \circ T$ is isomorphic to $T'$ (as an object of $\operatorname{Fun}( \prod _{i \in I} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_ i, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}' )$); moreover, $U$ is uniquely determined up to isomorphism. In this case, $T'$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}'$ as a $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete tensor product of $\{  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_ i \} _{i \in I }$ if and only if $U$ is an equivalence of $\infty $-categories. </p>
</article>
